# DIDA deployment inventory. Copy this file to deploy/hosts.conf (git-ignored)
# and list your own installations there.
#
# One line per instance; `deploy/prod.sh <name>` (or `all`) reads only this
# file, so adding a house is a line here, never a new script. Blank lines and
# `#` comments are ignored.
#
#   name      short handle you pass to prod.sh
#   ssh       ssh target (user@host, or an alias from ~/.ssh/config)
#   path      DIDA installation on that host
#   flags     comma-separated, or `-` for none:
#               apk  — ship the signed Android companion. The keystore lives
#                 ONLY on this dev box, so the APK is built here and travels as
#                 an artifact; only the house whose phones run it wants one.
#               demo — refresh the public demo (demo-dida.boskovic.biz) afterwards so
#                 it shows the frontend that just shipped.
#
# Unlike BABA, the source is NOT shipped from here: deploy/upgrade.sh fetches
# origin/main on the host, because that same script is what an operator runs on
# their own installation and the two paths must not drift apart. That is why the
# origin/main check in prod.sh has no escape hatch — an unpushed commit cannot
# reach a host at all, it would only be deployed as a surprise.
#
# ORDER MATTERS. `prod.sh all` deploys the first line here in front of you and
# lets the rest carry on in the background, so the list runs from what it costs
# most to leave stale down to what it costs least. Naming instances explicitly
# keeps them all in the foreground.
#
# name    ssh                   path              flags
home      root@192.0.2.20       /srv/dida         apk
cabin     cabin-dida            /srv/dida         -
